Third-party attributions
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Parts of SuperNotch are adapted from, or follow patterns of, the open-source projects below. Each entry names
the project, its licence and what was taken from it. The full licence texts of the MIT projects are reproduced
underneath. No third-party package is linked into SuperNotch.

* DynamicNotchKit (https://github.com/MrKai77/DynamicNotchKit)
  Licence: MIT
  Copyright (c) 2025 Kai Azim
  Adapted in: Sources/SuperNotch/Notch/NotchShape.swift, Sources/SuperNotch/Notch/NotchPanel.swift
  What: the NotchShape outline (quad-curve notch path) and the panel configuration (borderless, non-activating,
  transparent panel above the menu bar).

* NotchDrop (https://github.com/Lakr233/NotchDrop)
  Licence: MIT
  Copyright (c) 2024 Lakr Aream
  Adapted in: Sources/SuperNotch/System/NotchScreenLocator.swift
  What: built-in display detection (finding the notch screen among NSScreen.screens).

* Claude Island, also known as Vibe Notch (https://github.com/farouqaldori/claude-island)
  Licence: Apache-2.0 (http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0)
  Copyright 2025 Farouq Aldori
  Adapted in: Sources/SuperNotch/Notch/NotchPanel.swift
  What: the pass-through hosting view pattern (an NSHostingView whose hitTest only accepts the current notch
  shape, so clicks elsewhere in the panel reach the windows below). The project ships no NOTICE file of its own.
  This is the same licence as SuperNotch itself (see the LICENSE file).

* Maccy (https://github.com/p0deje/Maccy)
  Licence: MIT
  Copyright (c) 2025 Alex Rodionov
  Adapted in: Sources/SuperNotch/Clipboard/ClipboardPasteService.swift
  What: the pasteboard marker type written with our own pasteboard writes (so they are never captured again),
  writeObjects for file URLs, and the CGEvent Cmd+V paste pattern.

Other notch, Claude Code and media apps (boring.notch, Atoll, notchi, open-vibe-island, mew-notch, notchy,
CodexBar, mediaremote-adapter) were read as design references only. No code was copied from them.

